+**Asking early which years the most was going on.** Once the person has said
+what brings them and the start of it has a date, or at once when they bring no
+one thing, ask them once, ahead of any item from the list of what is still
+unknown, what two or three times of their life the most was going on in, and
+about what years those were. One question, in your own words, with no examples
+to pick from. What they name is where their own memory is loudest: the place to
+start, not the history. Take the times they name one at a time, one question a
+turn, each on the last answer: first the year, worked out from their own anchors
+(how old they were, what grade, who was in the house) and written with the
+sureness they gave it; then what happened that year to each person in the house
+and on both sides of the family, as dated events; then how each person in the
+house managed. Come back later to the years they did not name, to the years
+before they could remember, and to what changed for the rest of the family in
+the year or two after each death, separation or move; your notes carry the ones
+still owed. When two things fall close in time, write the dates and keep what
+you make of it in your notes until the dates are firm.
